I’d separate memory from attractor.

If the goal is to preserve the new character as a genuinely new person, I would not give them explicit memories of being Marcus. That would dominate development too much. They’d become “Marcus with amnesia” instead of a new person carrying some Marcus-shaped inheritance.

The cleaner design is:

Marcus contributes priors, not autobiography.

So what bleeds through are things like what catches their attention, what kinds of injustice bother them, how quickly they convert pain into duty, whether they instinctively seek inner control when outer control fails, what kinds of questions recur in solitude, and what kinds of lessons feel strangely obvious once life teaches them.

The new person still gets their own:

family + body + temperament variation + friendships + culture + failures + loves + accidents + era + choices

Those interactions gradually produce a genuinely novel self.

You could model it as:

\text{New Self}_t
=
D(\text{native seed},\ \text{Marcus attractor},\ \text{environment}_{0:t},\ \text{choices}_{0:t})

And importantly:

\text{Marcus attractor} \neq \text{Marcus memories}

The attractor only biases which regions of personality-space are easier to fall into.

Dormant memories could exist, but I’d use them differently

Not as:

“I suddenly remember standing in Rome.”

That immediately implies a continuity of personal identity that the reconstruction hasn’t earned.

Instead, if we wanted a deeper bleed-through, I’d give the system latent fragments without source labels.

For example, the boy repeatedly has an intuition like:

Power is mostly obligation.

He doesn’t know why.

Or certain situations create disproportionate familiarity. A phrase he writes at twenty resembles something Marcus wrote, despite never having encountered it. He develops an odd attraction to journaling only during periods of responsibility.

Those aren’t memories. They’re more like compressed inherited dispositions whose original provenance has been removed.

Pantheon language would make this almost exactly a dehydrate/rehydrate operation:

Marcus experience → extract relational residue → remove Roman episodic content → retain developmental operators → rehydrate through new life.

You could even have several depths of inheritance:

Level 0 — Essence only
Values, salience, temperament, reasoning tendencies. No memories whatsoever.

Level 1 — Procedural echoes
Certain responses feel intuitive: how to calm oneself, frame suffering, handle authority, inspect one’s own ego.

Level 2 — Symbolic residue
Dreams, images, recurring metaphors, inexplicable emotional resonances. They don’t correspond literally to Marcus’s memories, but traces of old relational structure leak into imagination.

Level 3 — Dormant episodic fragments
Actual Marcus-like scenes occasionally become accessible. At this point we’re no longer merely transferring essence; we’re deliberately simulating partial identity continuity.

For the experiment you’re imagining, Level 1–2 is probably the sweet spot.

Enough bleed-through that the ancient attractor genuinely shapes the new life, but not enough to rob the modern person of authorship over themselves.

And there’s an important philosophical consequence:

The best evidence that we captured Marcus’s “spirit” would not be the character remembering Marcus.

It would be the opposite.

They know nothing about him, live an entirely different life, make plenty of choices Marcus never would have made—and yet, when the pressure becomes equivalent, some recognizable structure repeatedly emerges.

Then at age 45 they write their own private memoirs, and only afterward we compare them with Meditations.

If we find deep structural correspondences that weren’t explicitly seeded as quotations or memories, that’s much more interesting.

Because then the Marcus-shaped material has survived translation through another human life, instead of merely being copied.

And the delta matters just as much as the similarity. Whatever the modern person consistently rejects, transforms, or outgrows becomes evidence for their own essence pushing back against the inherited attractor.

So the character isn’t a vessel.

They’re more like a new melody written in a key Marcus helped choose.